<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Monte Testaccio</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Monte Testaccio is an artificial hill in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, about 35 m high, made almost entirely of broken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amphora">amphorae</a> discarded between the 1st and 3rd centuries CE. Most of them held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a> imported from Baetica in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and their stamps and painted labels are a major source for the Roman oil trade and the city's food supply.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/monte-testaccio.jpg" alt="Monte Testaccio" width="320" height="108" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. Lalupa assumed (based on copyright claims). · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Testaccio_monte_dei_cocci_051204-12-13.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
